Domain Controller: A server that manages the Active Directory database, handles authentication and authorization, and maintains the overall security and integrity of the domain.
Domain Admin: A user with elevated privileges capable of managing all aspects of the domain, including AD objects, security policies, and resources.

Group Policy Objects (GPOs) are essential tools for administrators to enforce policies and manage configurations in a Windows environment. By using GPOs, 
administrators can centrally control settings for users and computers, enhancing security, compliance, and operational efficiency within an Active Directory domain.

OU stands for Organizational Unit. Organizational Units are containers within an AD domain that can hold users, groups, computers, and other OUs.

If you want to set up security features in OU > create a GPO and add it in that domain (OU)

